# Compliance memo — wheel event type under UI Events

## 1. Direct answer

**The event type is `wheel`, dispatched as a `WheelEvent`.** There is no event type called "document wheel" in any edition of UI Events; a "document wheel" is a `wheel` event whose propagation path reaches `Document` (i.e. it is targeted at an element inside the document and bubbles, or is targeted at the `Document` itself when nothing else is under the cursor). A user agent must dispatch **`wheel`** — not `mousewheel`, not `DOMMouseScroll`, both of which are non-standard and appear in no W3C or WHATWG specification.

Normative sentence, verbatim:

> "A user agent MUST dispatch this event when a mouse wheel has been rotated around any axis, or when an equivalent input device (such as a mouse-ball, certain tablets or touchpads, etc.) has emulated such an action. Depending on the platform and input device, diagonal wheel deltas MAY be delivered either as a single wheel event with multiple non-zero axes or as separate wheel events for each non-zero axis."

## 2. Which text you are citing — this matters and is the second half of the ask

The wheel section has **moved**. As of the current W3C publication of UI Events (`https://www.w3.org/TR/uievents/`, fetched 2026-11-23, sha256 8ded5eaf4f19…), §1.1.1 "Mouse and Wheel Events" reads, in full:

> "The Mouse Events and Wheel Events section of this specification have been moved to the Pointer Events specification [pointerevents4]."

Consequences a reviewer must record:

1. **In the current `/TR/uievents/` document, UI Events itself no longer contains a wheel dispatch requirement.** The only surviving generic obligation is the conformance rule: *"A conforming browser MUST dispatch events appropriate to the given EventTarget when the conditions defined for that event type have been met."* (§1.2 Conformance, normative.) Applied to wheel, the "conditions defined for that event type" now live in Pointer Events 4.
2. **The normative home of the wheel event type is Pointer Events 4** (`https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-pointerevents4-20260616/`, Working Draft, Recommendation track; §6.2 wheel events, with the type subsection at §6.2.1 and cancelability at §6.2.2). Its Status of This Document states: *"It also includes the Mouse and Wheel Events, previously in the [UIEVENTS] specification."* Note the maturity difference: this is a **Working Draft**, not a Recommendation — a compliance citation must say so, because a WD is explicitly "inappropriate to cite … as other than a work in progress."
3. **The UI Events editor's draft still carries the wheel text** at `https://w3c.github.io/uievents/split/wheel-events.html` (§3.1 Interface WheelEvent; §3.2.1 wheel; §3.2.2 cancelability of wheel events). This is the convenient stable quote source for the UI Events lineage.
4. **The requirement text is unchanged across editions.** The 2015 WD (`https://www.w3.org/TR/2015/WD-uievents-20150428/`) has the identical MUST-dispatch sentence and identical default-action sentence. So the answer `wheel` is not draft-sensitive even though the section's *address* is.

**Recommended citation string for the review record:**

> UI Events (W3C), §1.1.1 "Mouse and Wheel Events" (section relocated); wheel event type defined in Pointer Events 4, W3C Working Draft 16 June 2026, §6.2.1 (`wheel`, `WheelEvent`) and §6.2.2 (cancelability); equivalent text in UI Events WD 28 April 2015 and in the UI Events editor's draft §3.2.

## 3. What the section actually says (normative content, quoted)

### 3.1 Dispatch trigger (MUST)
Quoted in §1 above. Two MAY-level relaxations are part of the requirement and must not be read as violations: diagonal deltas may arrive as one multi-axis event **or** as separate per-axis events, and delta magnitude may scale with wheel speed (*"the speed that the wheel has been turned can affect the delta values, with a faster speed producing a higher delta value"*).

### 3.2 Default action and cancelation
> "The typical default action of the wheel event type is to scroll (or in some cases, zoom) the document by the indicated amount. If this event is canceled, the implementation MUST NOT scroll or zoom the document (or perform whatever other implementation-specific default action is associated with this event type)."

So: default action = scroll/zoom; cancelation via `preventDefault()` is normative and binding on the UA.

### 3.3 Cancelability is conditional — do not state it as an invariant
> "Calling preventDefault on a wheel event can prevent or otherwise interrupt scrolling. For maximum scroll performance, a user agent may not wait for each wheel event associated with the scroll to be processed to see if it will be canceled. In such cases the user agent should generate wheel events whose cancelable property is false, indicating that preventDefault cannot be used to prevent or interrupt scrolling. Otherwise cancelable will be true."
> "In particular, a user agent should generate only uncancelable wheel events when it observes that there are no non-passive listeners for the event."

Practical meaning: `event.cancelable` is **true only when the UA can afford to wait**, which in practice means when at least one non-passive `wheel` listener exists. A `preventDefault()` call inside a passive listener is a no-op (browsers log a console warning). This is SHOULD-level, so a UA emitting only cancelable wheel events is not non-conforming, but a UA emitting `cancelable: false` for a page with only passive listeners is explicitly conforming.

### 3.4 Delta attributes and deltaMode
> "deltaX, of type double, readonly — In user agents where the default action of the wheel event is to scroll, the value MUST be the measurement along the x-axis (in pixels, lines, or pages) to be scrolled in the case where the event is not cancelled. Otherwise, this is an implementation-specific measurement … The un-initialized value of this attribute MUST be 0.0."

The same construction is repeated for `deltaY` and `deltaZ` (all three: un-initialized value MUST be `0.0`). `deltaMode` selects the unit: `DOM_DELTA_PIXEL = 0x00`, `DOM_DELTA_LINE = 0x01`, `DOM_DELTA_PAGE = 0x02`. Because all three deltas may be non-zero simultaneously, per-axis handling is required, not optional.

### 3.5 Targeting — Pointer Events 4 addition relevant to "document wheel"
> "A user agent MUST create a wheel event transaction when the first wheel event is fired, so that all subsequent wheel events within a implementation-specific amount of time can be targetted at the same element. A wheel event transaction is series of wheel events that are associated with a single user gesture. The wheel event transaction MUST have an associated event target that is the topmost event target at the time the first wheel event occurs in the group."

This is the clause that decides what a "document wheel" actually is: the target is the **topmost element** at the moment the gesture begins (commonly `<html>`/`<body>` for a wheel over empty page area, or the `Document` itself when there is no element hit), and the whole gesture keeps that target even if the page scrolls underneath the cursor. A listener on `document` sees it through bubbling; a listener on `window` sees it last. Attaching to `document` therefore observes every wheel gesture, which is why the spec bothers to pin the target — it prevents mid-gesture target churn.

### 3.6 What the section does NOT say
- It does not define `mousewheel` (IE/WebKit legacy) or `DOMMouseScroll` (Firefox legacy, removed in modern Firefox). Their absence is the whole of their status: non-standard, non-conforming to rely on.
- It does not require `wheel` to fire for scrollbar dragging, keyboard scrolling, or programmatic `scrollTo()` — those produce `scroll` events, not `wheel`.
- It does not say `wheel` is the only event a UA may dispatch; a UA *may* additionally dispatch legacy types. Required ≠ exclusive.

## 4. Implementation checklist (frontend)

```js
// 1. Feature-detect the standard interface, never the legacy ones.
const hasWheelEvent = typeof WheelEvent !== 'undefined';

// 2. Register non-passive ONLY if you intend to cancel.
//    Non-passive document-level wheel listeners delay scroll and
//    force the UA down the 'wait to see if cancelled' path (§3.3).
const LINE_HEIGHT_PX = 16; // your own line-height, not a spec value

function onWheel(event) {
  // event.type === 'wheel'; event instanceof WheelEvent
  const { deltaX, deltaY, deltaZ, deltaMode, ctrlKey } = event;

  // Pinch-zoom arrives as wheel + ctrlKey. Never hijack it.
  if (ctrlKey) return;

  // Normalize to pixels using deltaMode (0/1/2 per §3.4).
  let scale = 1;
  if (deltaMode === WheelEvent.DOM_DELTA_LINE) scale = LINE_HEIGHT_PX;
  else if (deltaMode === WheelEvent.DOM_DELTA_PAGE) scale = window.innerHeight;

  const dx = deltaX * scale;
  const dy = deltaY * scale;
  const dz = deltaZ * scale;

  if (!shouldHijack(event)) return;      // leave native scroll alone

  event.preventDefault();                // no-op if passive; see §3.3
  applyCustomScroll(dx, dy, dz);
}

document.addEventListener('wheel', onWheel, { passive: false });
// Use { passive: true } for telemetry/analytics listeners.

// 3. Do NOT do this — non-standard, no spec backing:
// document.addEventListener('mousewheel', h);
// document.addEventListener('DOMMouseScroll', h);
```

Additional engineering constraints that follow from the quoted text:

- **Scroll-jacking is an accessibility hazard.** If you cancel `wheel`, you must keep keyboard (Arrow / PageUp / PageDown / Home / End), scrollbar dragging, and assistive-technology scrolling functional, and honour `prefers-reduced-motion` for any animated scroll you substitute. The spec's default action is scroll/zoom; you are overriding it, so you own the equivalent.
- **Never rely on `deltaY` sign magnitude being comparable across devices or platforms** — the spec makes the non-scroll case explicitly implementation-specific, and speed-dependent even in the scroll case.
- **Handle `deltaZ`** even though it is rare on mice; the interface defines it and some devices emit it.
- **Composition with pointer events:** a `wheel` gesture does not produce `pointerdown`/`pointerup`; do not attempt to correlate them.

## 5. Verification procedure for the reviewer

1. In any modern browser console, on a page with no handlers:
   `document.addEventListener('wheel', e => console.log(e.type, e.constructor.name, e.cancelable, e.deltaMode, e.deltaX, e.deltaY), {passive:false})`
   then rotate the wheel. Expect `wheel`, `WheelEvent`, `cancelable: true` (a non-passive listener now exists), `deltaMode` of `0` (or `1` on Firefox with a line-mode mouse setting).
2. Repeat with the listener registered `{passive:true}`; the UA may report `cancelable: false` — that is §3.3 conforming behaviour, not a bug.
3. Confirm `mousewheel` never fires in Firefox and that no W3C/WHATWG spec defines it; confirm `DOMMouseScroll` does not fire in modern Firefox.
4. Confirm `wheel` does not fire when the page is scrolled by keyboard or by dragging the scrollbar.

## 6. One-line answer for the review record

> A conforming user agent must dispatch the **`wheel`** event (`WheelEvent`) when a mouse wheel is rotated or an equivalent device emulates it; the requirement text now lives in Pointer Events 4 §6.2 because UI Events §1.1.1 states the Mouse and Wheel Events section was moved there, and the identical MUST-dispatch sentence is preserved from UI Events WD 2015 and the UI Events editor's draft §3.2. Legacy `mousewheel` and `DOMMouseScroll` are non-standard and must not be cited as conforming.
